WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
A pivot often requires aggregation.

00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:07.052
If only one source row maps to each output cell, the reshape can preserve that value directly.

00:00:07.052 --> 00:00:13.807
But if multiple source rows map to one cell, PostgreSQL needs an aggregate such as SUM, COUNT, MAX, or AVG.

00:00:13.807 --> 00:00:18.127
That aggregation changes the level of detail, so it must be chosen deliberately.

00:00:18.127 --> 00:00:23.337
Connect this idea to a BI dashboard turning monthly categories into presentation columns.

00:00:23.337 --> 00:00:27.752
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:34.277
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:39.272
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:44.080
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
